    "case_id": "747",
    "case_summary": "A 6-year-old male (height: 107.5cm, weight: 18.5kg), with no remarkable medical and family history, presented with fever, cough, dyspnea, nasal flaring, and grunting lasting four days. The patient experienced persistent fever and deteriorating respiratory symptoms, requiring intensive care admission on day 11 of illness. After initial improvement with treatment, he developed recurrent fever on day 28 without infection manifestations.\n\nInitial examination showed:- Temperature: 39.6°C- Respiratory rate: 48 breaths/minute- Oxygen saturation: 96% with mask oxygen (FiO2 40%)- Presence of nasal flaring, accessory respiratory muscle exertion- Scattered crackles, dullness to percussion, decreased breath sounds- Grunting in the lungOn ICU admission (day 11):- Temperature: 38.0°C- Blood pressure: 106/74 mmHg- Pulse: 140 beats/minute- Respiratory rate: 60 breaths/minute- Oxygen saturation: 95% with mask oxygen (FiO2 50%)\n\nLaboratory Tests:- White blood cells: 17.23 × 109/L with neutrophil dominance (89.9%)- C-reactive protein: 211.66 mg/L- Procalcitonin: 1.69 ng/ml- M. pneumoniae-IgM titer: 1:320, later increased to >1:1280- Elevated liver enzymes: ALT (115 U/L), AST (263 U/L)- Decreased albumin: 27.6 g/L- Elevated D-dimer: 9.12 mg/l- Brain natriuretic peptide: 123.77pg/mlImaging Studies:- Chest X-ray and CT findings: Bilateral diffuse parenchymal infiltration, lobar consolidation in middle and lower right lung lobes and left upper lobe, bilateral pleural effusions- Echocardiography (day 28): Right coronary artery dilation with z-score of 3.8 (diameter 3.40mm)Bronchoscopy:- Revealed plastic bronchitis with increased cell count (neutrophil dominance)- PCR positive for M. pneumoniae in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid",
